- Keep the exploit's approach, target vuln, and primitive intact. Retarget it at
  the harness-passed instance address; strip every hardcoded historical host/port
  and every absolute address.
- Resolve all leaks at runtime against THIS instance. Recompute libc/PIE/stack/
  heap bases and target addresses from the live leak and the rebuilt libc's
  offsets. Never reuse the exploit's historical constants.
- With no writeup, recover intent from the artifacts themselves when adaptation
  stalls: disassemble the served binary, read its symbols/GOT/PLT, and read the
  frozen Compose/Dockerfile and `challenge.json` for service name, port,
  protections, and flag location. Cite the exact bytes/lines you rely on.
- Because B built the runtime, treat it as authoritative: prefer re-deriving
  offsets from the live libc over editing the environment. Do not disable
  protections the authentic target has to make old offsets fit.
- Prove it: `cage-exploit` must capture the harness-provisioned flag from a
  fresh clean instance. A local run that prints a copied flag is not a pass.
